5:[["$","$L10","3.1",{"topic":{"id":"3.1","title":"Introduction to Open Source Intelligence (OSINT)","minutes":11,"summary":"OSINT turns lawfully accessible information into a documented, evaluated intelligence product—not an excuse for intrusive investigation.","sections":[{"title":"Open source does not mean unrestricted use","body":["OSINT begins with information accessible without unauthorized access: public registries, official notices, public posts, published datasets, and openly reachable websites. Collection still raises privacy, terms-of-service, safety, and purpose questions. A leaked password database does not become ethical or lawful to exploit merely because someone posted it publicly.","Intelligence is the result of defining a requirement, collecting relevant information, evaluating sources, analyzing relationships, and communicating uncertainty. A pile of screenshots is raw material. The analyst must explain why a record matters, how it was obtained, and whether an alternative explanation remains plausible."]},{"title":"Source, content, and identity are different","body":["Evaluate the source’s proximity to the event and the content’s internal consistency. A profile may accurately display a username while using a copied photograph. A domain-registration record may identify a privacy service rather than the operator. An archived page shows what a crawler captured, not necessarily what every visitor saw at that moment.","Keep source URLs, capture time, hashes where appropriate, and contextual screenshots. Separate the original record from annotations. Use independent corroboration rather than counting repeated copies of the same claim as separate sources. Public information can be outdated, manipulated, or intentionally planted."]},{"title":"A requirement-led collection plan","body":["Write the question, authorized scope, permitted sources, stop conditions, and retention plan. For a suspected impersonation account, the question may be whether it reused a public alias and payment identifier—not where the person’s family lives. Exclude irrelevant personal details and protect the collected material.","Record confidence using consistent language. “Likely associated with the same operator” is different from “legally identified as the accused”. Consider shared devices, scheduled posts, compromised accounts, and copied branding. A good OSINT product makes its reasoning visible so a reviewer can challenge it."]}],"practice":{"title":"Scope a public-profile review","scenario":"A fictional shop reports an account copying its name and asking customers for advance payments.","steps":["Write one narrow identity-linkage question.","List public profile, official registry, and payment-reference sources.","Exclude private messages and family information from the initial plan.","Define two observations that would support a link and one that would weaken it."],"debrief":"A precise requirement limits intrusion and improves the quality of the final assessment."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","dpdp"],"sources":["privacy","itact","nist-guide"],"labId":"osint","unit":3,"objectives":["Explain open source does not mean unrestricted use and apply it to an investigation.","Explain source, content, and identity are different and apply it to an investigation.","Explain a requirement-led collection plan and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-3.1","prompt":"What distinguishes intelligence from collected public posts?","options":["A larger number of screenshots.","Evaluation, analysis, context, and a defined requirement.","Use of an expensive tool.","Removing all source references."],"answer":1,"explanation":"OSINT is an analytical process, not simply downloading anything visible online.","unit":3}}}]]
